Since $T$ is a linear transformation, we have that

$$
\begin{aligned}T(kv+hw)=T(kv)+T(hw)=kT(v)+hT(w)\end{aligned}
$$

for any vectors $v$ and $w.$ Therefore,

$$
\begin{aligned}T(4{v}_{1}-5{v}_{2}) & =4T({v}_{1})-5T({v}_{2}) \\ [\begin{aligned}-13 \\ 6\end{aligned}] & =4[\begin{aligned}-2 \\ 4\end{aligned}]-5T({v}_{2}) \\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =4[\begin{aligned}-2 \\ 4\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}-13 \\ 6\end{aligned}] \\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}-8 \\ 16\end{aligned}]-[\begin{aligned}-13 \\ 6\end{aligned}] \\ 5T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}5 \\ 10\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{2}) & =\frac{1}{5}[\begin{aligned}5 \\ 10\end{aligned}] \\ T({v}_{2}) & =[\begin{aligned}1 \\ 2\end{aligned}].\end{aligned}
$$

